title: Slow log configuration
tags: [[Slow log]] Configuration
kind: reference
summary: The thresholds: 2 s for what writes, 100 ms for what only reads, 100 000 keys whatever the time; 0 turns the log off.

In the daemon's `~/.config/metafolder/daemon/config.toml` ([[Daemon configuration]]):

```
[settings]
slow-operation-threshold-ms = 2000
slow-read-threshold-ms = 100
slow-operation-reads-threshold = 100000
```

and in the GUI's `~/.config/metafolder/gui/config.toml`, `slow-operation-threshold-ms`
alone, with its own value.

|!Key |!Default |!Meaning |
|`slow-operation-threshold-ms` |`2000` |an operation taking longer is logged. `0` turns the log off entirely: nothing is timed, no file is created. There is no "log everything" value: `1` is one |
|`slow-read-threshold-ms` |`100` |daemon only: the threshold of an operation that ''wrote nothing'' — a query, a listing, a read of the log. `0` holds reads to the main threshold |
|`slow-operation-reads-threshold` |`100000` |daemon only: an operation that read at least this many keys is logged ''however quick it was''. `0` leaves it to time alone |

Why a separate read threshold: a query is felt at a tenth of a second — the GUI's
finder sends one per keystroke — long before a write or a watcher flush is, and a
single threshold was either blind to queries or flooded by flushes. What wrote is
not decided per route: the store's write transaction says so as it commits, so any
operation that never committed is a read, and a new route needs no
classification.

Why a key-count threshold: a duration depends on the machine and its load, a count
of keys does not, so an operation that reads a large part of a repository is
caught even on a machine fast enough to hide it. Every bounded read stays far
below the default.
